  "marks": "Mastering: Argues with precision, using named sources (thermopolia, stepping stones, forum colonnade, inscriptions, Insula of Diana at Ostia for comparison). Evaluates both the value of the evidence (exceptional preservation, wide range of building types, commercial and domestic detail) and its limits (both are frozen in AD 79; both are Italian towns, not the capital; Pompeii may not represent the poorest Romans). Reaches a clear, supported judgement.\nSecure: Uses two or more named examples from Pompeii or Herculaneum and makes a clear argument about usefulness, with at least one evaluative point.\nDeveloping: Describes what Pompeii reveals but does not evaluate; argument is one-sided or unsupported.\nEmerging: General statements about Pompeii without named evidence."
 },
 {
  "text": "How far do the physical remains of Pompeii give us a complete picture of Roman city life? In your answer, refer to both the evidence and its limitations.",
  "type": "8 marks — Essay (Extended Response)",
  "marks": "Mastering: Sustained argument using a range of named sources (e.g. thermopolia, forum layout, stepping stones, wall-paintings, graffiti inscriptions, water tower/castellum aquae). Considers both strength of evidence (extraordinary preservation; wide range of building types; evidence from both wealthy and ordinary households) and limitations (single moment in time — AD 79; not the capital; limited evidence for the very poorest; bias towards surviving stone buildings). Reaches a supported judgement about how complete the picture is, perhaps noting that Ostia or literary sources like Juvenal fill some of the gaps.\nSecure: Clear argument using several named examples; evaluates both strength and limitation; reaches a judgement.\nDeveloping: Describes the evidence but does not evaluate it critically; argument is largely one-sided.\nEmerging: General descriptive points about Pompeii without use of specific named evidence."
 }
]

