=== L3 The Roman Domus ===
id: 9e3dbd26-012b-47df-9ac8-b73b86edaa3a
slug: the-roman-domus
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:43:23.323698+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 21

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Inside a wealthy Roman town house — the atrium, the peristyle and the rooms that displayed status.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">The Domus: A Home Designed to Impress</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">In a Roman city, where you lived told the world everything about who you were. The wealthy Roman town house &mdash; the <dfn class="term" data-def="A Roman private house of the wealthier sort, typically built around a central atrium and peristyle garden.">domus</dfn> &mdash; was not simply a private retreat. It was an arena of social performance. Its rooms were arranged to guide visitors on a carefully choreographed journey from the public street to the innermost spaces of family life, with each zone communicating a message about the owner&rsquo;s wealth, taste and status. The House of Menander and the House of Octavius Quartio at Pompeii are two of the best-preserved examples, and they allow us to read that social performance in remarkable detail.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Entering the Domus: The Fauces and Atrium</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">A visitor approaching a wealthy domus from the street would pass through a narrow entrance corridor called the fauces (meaning throat). This passage was often decorated with mosaics warning visitors &mdash; sometimes with an image of a chained dog and the words <em>cave canem</em> (beware of the dog) &mdash; that not everyone was welcome. Beyond the fauces opened the <dfn class="term" data-def="The central hall of a Roman domus, open to the sky through a rectangular hole in the roof, with a basin beneath to catch rainwater.">atrium</dfn>, the most public room of the house. The atrium had a rectangular opening in the roof called the compluvium, which let in light and air. Directly below this was the <dfn class="term" data-def="The shallow rectangular pool set into the floor of the Roman atrium, positioned to catch rainwater falling through the compluvium above.">impluvium</dfn>, a shallow pool that collected rainwater. The impluvium was sometimes lined with mosaic and often decorated with a small fountain.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">The atrium served as both reception hall and waiting room. The patron of the household received clients here each morning during the salutatio, the formal morning greeting, when those who owed political or financial loyalty to the householder came to pay their respects. Visitors waiting in the atrium were deliberately kept in view of the household&rsquo;s wealth: bronze furniture, fine floor mosaics, and family portraits (called imagines) would all be on display. The whole arrangement was designed to make an impression before a word was spoken.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: what is the name of the opening in the roof of the atrium, and what is the pool beneath it called? Write one sentence explaining how each contributed to the function of the room.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The atrium was the main public room of a Roman domus. It had a central roof opening (the compluvium) that let in light, and a pool beneath (the impluvium) to catch rainwater. This arrangement also served as a visual centrepiece for impressing clients during the morning salutatio.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">The Tablinum and the Triclinium</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Behind the atrium, directly aligned with the entrance, stood the <dfn class="term" data-def="The owner&rsquo;s study or office in a Roman domus, positioned between the atrium and the peristyle, where business and client meetings were conducted.">tablinum</dfn>. This was the office of the householder, where the paterfamilias conducted business, received important clients, and stored household records. Its position, opening onto both the atrium and the garden beyond, allowed the owner to be visible to visitors while remaining in a position of authority. The tablinum was often richly decorated with wall paintings to emphasise the owner&rsquo;s sophistication and wealth.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">The grandest room for social entertainment was the <dfn class="term" data-def="The formal dining room of a Roman house, where the evening dinner party (cena) was held and guests reclined on couches arranged around low tables.">triclinium</dfn>. Guests reclined on three couches &mdash; giving the room its name from the Greek for &ldquo;three couches&rdquo; &mdash; arranged in a U-shape around low tables. At the top of the arrangement lay the most honoured position, the lectus medius summus, where the host would place his most important guest. The triclinium was decorated to maximise its visual effect: the House of Menander at Pompeii preserves a triclinium with black wall paintings framed in red and yellow borders, of a high quality that demonstrates the household&rsquo;s wealth and cultural sophistication.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">The Peristyle Garden</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Beyond the tablinum, the house opened onto the <dfn class="term" data-def="A colonnade-enclosed garden at the rear of a Roman domus, often containing flower beds, statues, fountains and a triclinium opening onto it.">peristyle</dfn>, a colonnaded garden at the rear of the house. The peristyle was the private heart of the household, used by family rather than clients. It typically contained flower beds, statues, a fountain, and sometimes a fishpond. The colonnades provided shaded walkways and framed the garden into a formal composition. In the House of Octavius Quartio at Pompeii, the garden was exceptionally elaborate: it contained a long ornamental water channel, wall paintings of hunting scenes and mythological subjects, and a garden dining area. The paintings from this house vividly illustrate how wealthy Pompeians decorated their outdoor spaces with mythological imagery as a display of cultural knowledge and artistic patronage.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n12">The orientation of the domus was not accidental. The arrangement from street to atrium to tablinum to peristyle created a single visual axis: a visitor standing at the door could look all the way through to the garden. This axial design was deliberately theatrical, drawing the eye through the house and giving every section its moment in the viewer&rsquo;s line of sight. Even in a private home, the Romans thought architecturally about movement, sight lines and the performance of status.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Wall Paintings and Mosaics: Art as Display</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">Roman wall paintings in Pompeii have survived in extraordinary condition because they were protected by the volcanic ash that buried the city. Art historians have identified four main styles of Pompeian wall painting. The Second Style (roughly 80&ndash;15 BC) used painted architectural illusions to make walls appear to open onto colonnades, columns and landscapes &mdash; a trompe l&rsquo;oeil effect that made rooms seem larger than they were. The Third and Fourth Styles favoured smaller mythological scenes set within elaborate architectural frames. These paintings communicated the owner&rsquo;s education and cultural awareness: mythological subjects required the viewer to recognise the stories of the gods and heroes, and identifying them correctly was a sign of cultivated learning.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n14">Floor mosaics were equally important as status symbols. The famous <em>Cave Canem</em> mosaic from the entrance of the House of the Tragic Poet at Pompeii shows a chained dog with the Latin warning below. Its placing at the entrance was a witty display of wealth and sophistication: the mosaic demonstrates that the household could afford fine floor decoration while simultaneously keeping unwanted visitors away with a gentle joke. Mosaics throughout the house served not just as floor covering but as miniature works of art, with the finest examples &mdash; like the celebrated Alexander mosaic found at the House of the Faun at Pompeii &mdash; requiring extraordinary skill and expense to produce.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n15" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name the rooms of a Roman domus in the order a visitor would encounter them, from the street inwards. For each room, write one sentence about what it was used for.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The layout of the domus moved from public to private: fauces (entrance passage) &rarr; atrium (public reception hall) &rarr; tablinum (owner&rsquo;s office) &rarr; triclinium (dining room) &rarr; peristyle (private garden). Each zone was decorated and furnished according to how many visitors were expected to see it.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: Was the Domus Purely a Private Home?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n16">Many students assume that the domus was primarily a private, domestic space. In fact, a wealthy Roman domus was intensely public in character. The morning salutatio brought dozens of clients into the atrium; business was conducted in the tablinum; the triclinium hosted dinner parties that were social and political events as much as meals. The house was the primary arena through which a Roman aristocrat exercised patronage, built political alliances, and displayed his wealth and culture. The boundary between public and private that modern people assume was much less fixed in the Roman world. This is why the decoration of every room &mdash; even the entrance passage &mdash; was so carefully considered: every space was potentially on display.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n18">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">The Roman domus was arranged from public to private: fauces &rarr; atrium (with compluvium and impluvium) &rarr; tablinum &rarr; triclinium &rarr; peristyle garden. Each space communicated the owner&rsquo;s status to the visitors it was designed to receive.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Wall paintings and mosaics were not merely decoration &mdash; they were displays of wealth and cultural knowledge, with mythological scenes requiring an educated audience to appreciate them fully.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">The domus was as much a public performance space as a home: the morning salutatio (client greeting) and the cena (dinner party) made the house an arena of social and political life.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n17">When a question asks you to <strong>explain</strong> what a feature of the domus reveals about Roman values, make sure you move from the physical detail to the social meaning. Do not just say &ldquo;the atrium had an impluvium&rdquo; &mdash; explain <em>why</em> that mattered: it was the focal point of the most public room in the house, used to impress clients during the morning greeting. The biggest examiner complaint is answers that describe what a room looked like without saying what it tells us about Roman attitudes to status, social hierarchy, or the relationship between public and private life.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
